<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dompu Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1545 CE – 1958 CE (approx.) · capital Dompu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small sultanate in eastern Sumbawa, conquered by Majapahit in the 14th century and lasting into independent Indonesia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Dompo is listed in the Javanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarakretagama">Nagarakretagama</a> among the dependencies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, and an expedition to it is recorded in the mid-14th century. The register dates the sultanate from 1545, when its rulers are said to have adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, influenced by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gowa-sultanate">Makassar</a> and Bima.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Dompu was an agricultural state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and horses, whose people spoke the Bima language. It lived in the shadow of its larger neighbour Bima and of Gowa, and from the 17th century of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a>, which made treaties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa">Sumbawa</a> rulers. The eruption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tambora">Mount Tambora</a> in 1815 devastated the region, destroying the neighbouring kingdoms of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tambora">Tambora</a> and Pekat.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Dompu remained a self-governing state under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule into the 20th century. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-independence">Indonesian independence</a> the sultanates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa">Sumbawa</a> were dissolved, Dompu becoming a regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-nusa-tenggara">West Nusa Tenggara</a> in 1958.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1545 CE – 1958 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden